<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Function_Minimum_1</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The quadratic function</p>
<p>\[ f(x) = {@p@} \]</p>
<p>has a positive coefficient of \(x^2\), so its graph is a parabola that opens upwards and its turning point is a <strong>minimum</strong>. Find the turning point by completing the square. Do not use calculus.</p>
<p>(a) Find the \(x\)-coordinate of the turning point.</p>
<div>\(x =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) Find the minimum value of \(f(x)\).</p>
<div>Minimum value of \(f(x) =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Completing the square</strong> rewrites a quadratic \(a x^2 + bx + c\) in the form \(a(x - h)^2 + k\). [[if test="is(a#1)"]]First take the factor \({@a@}\) out of the two \(x\) terms. [[/if]]Then use \(x^2 + qx = \left(x + \dfrac{q}{2}\right)^2 - \left(\dfrac{q}{2}\right)^2\): halve the coefficient of \(x\) inside the bracket, square the bracket, and subtract the square of that half.</p>
<p>\[\begin{array}{rcl} f(x) &amp;=&amp; {@p@} \\ &amp;=&amp; {@step1@} \\ &amp;=&amp; {@step2@} \\ &amp;=&amp; {@step3@} \end{array}\]</p>
<p>A square is never negative, so \({@sq@} \ge 0\) for every \(x\), and it equals \(0\) only when \(x = {@h@}\). [[if test="is(a#1)"]]Multiplying by the positive number \({@a@}\) keeps this true. [[/if]]So \(f(x) \ge {@k@}\) for every \(x\), with equality at \(x = {@h@}\).</p>
<p>The turning point is therefore \(({@h@},\ {@k@})\). (a) Its \(x\)-coordinate is \(x = {@h@}\). (b) The minimum value of \(f(x)\) is \({@k@}\).</p>
<p>Check: substituting \(x = {@h@}\) into the original expression gives \(f({@h@}) = {@k@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber/>
    <stackversion>
      <text>2026063000</text>
    </stackversion>
    <questionvariables>
      <text>/* f(x) = a(x - h)^2 + k with a in {1,2,3} (always positive, so the turning point is a
   minimum on every seed), h and k non-zero integers. h # 0 keeps a non-zero x term. */
a: rand([1, 2, 3]);
h: rand_with_prohib(-5, 5, [0]);
k: rand_with_prohib(-9, 9, [0]);
b: -2*a*h;
c: a*h^2 + k;
p: a*x^2 + b*x + c;
/* Worked-solution display steps. */
step1: a*(x^2 - 2*h*x) + c;
step2: a*((x - h)^2 - h^2) + c;
step3: a*(x - h)^2 + k;
sq: (x - h)^2;
tax: h;
tak: k;
/* Diagnostics.
   wx_sign: x = b/(2a), the sign error from reading (x - h)^2 as zero at x = -h. Distinct from h since h # 0.
   wk_sign: the constant added instead of subtracted, c + a*h^2 = k + 2*a*h^2. Distinct from k since h # 0.
   prt2 node 2 compares ans2 with f(ans1) (value consistent with a wrong x). Its test uses
   fwrong = f(-h) = k + 4*a*h^2, which is neither k nor wk_sign. The both-wrong test uses
   ans1 = h+1, ans2 = k-1: f(h+1) = k+a and wk_sign are never k-1. */
wx_sign: -h;
wk_sign: c + a*h^2;
fwrong: ev(p, x = -h);</text>
    </questionvariables>
    <specificfeedback format="html">
      <text/>
    </specificfeedback>
    <questionnote format="html">
      <text>\(f(x) = {@p@} = {@step3@}\): turning point \(({@h@}, {@k@})\), a minimum.</text>
    </questionnote>
